PLAIN-SPEAK QUESTION

Task-1 taught us that a small amount of neural control over recruitment can change real developmental decisions without usually hurting what the organism gets done.

But we also learned that tiny local differences can send the organism down a different long-term path, and one of our Task-1 absolute score rules was sometimes impossible even for the hand-written parent.

So LU-2G stops squeezing the same task.

We give the same developmental organism a different kind of job.

Instead of one calculation per request, each request now needs two calculations in sequence, where the second calculation depends on the first.

We cut arrivals in half so we are testing a new task structure, not simply doubling the workload.

The neural network still cannot see which task, which program, which stage, or which phase it is in.

It only gets the same local cell observation it always had.

Then we compare 25-percent learned recruitment authority against the exact hand-written recruiter using the same worlds and random numbers.

If the hybrid keeps up on this new compositional task and actually causes different recruitment decisions, that is stronger evidence that the learned local behavior is not merely tuned to Task-1.

If the exact hand-written organism itself cannot run Task-2 reliably, we record that as a Task-2 design failure rather than blaming the neural controller.

